        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8932/0 Adenomyoma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63916,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F33.Y &amp; XH4ZH4 Benign neoplasm of other specified female genital organs &amp; Adenomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63917,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> adenomyomatous polyp; endometrioid-type adenomyoma; polypoid adenomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63918,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63919,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours are localized predominantly in the uterine corpus and occasionally in the uterine cervix. They may be polypoid, intramural, or serosal-based lesions. They may also occur in the broad ligament {16990713; 19047912; 30339971; 10907166}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63920,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Adenomyomas occur over a wide age range. Most patients present with abnormal vaginal bleeding.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:25:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63921,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No epidemiological data are available.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63922,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63923,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Some cases are associated with underlying adenomyosis and represent a localized form of adenomyosis; in one study, adenomyosis was present in 30.8% of cases {16990713}. One study found that these tumours lacked mutations in exon&nbsp;2 of <em>MED12</em> (characteristic of uterine leiomyomas) {30339971}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63924,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>In one large series, the tumours measured 0.3&ndash;17&nbsp;cm in maximum dimension {10907166}. They are nodular or polypoid tumours with a predominantly solid white whorled cut surface, sometimes with small cysts.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:25:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63925,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenomyomas of the uterine corpus are nodules or polyps composed of glands and cysts lined by bland endometrioid-type epithelium surrounded by endometrioid-type stroma and smooth muscle, with smooth muscle typically predominating {10907166; 16990713}. Various metaplasias may occur within the epithelial component, including tubal, mucinous, and squamous. The endometrioid-type stroma surrounds the glands but may be inconspicuous throughout much of the lesion. The smooth muscle component is typically morphologically bland without increased cellularity, often with a component of thick-walled blood vessels, but occasionally it is cellular, has an epithelioid appearance, or contains occasional bizarre nuclei without mitotic activity {10907166; 16990713; 23926192}. It is not uncommon for a minor component of smooth muscle to be present in the stroma of endometrial polyps, and these cases should not be termed adenomyomas. Immunohistochemically, the endometrioid-type stromal component is typically positive with CD10, and the smooth muscle component with desmin, SMA, and h-caldesmon.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63926,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 256,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:10:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
